{
  format: 1
  name: surface-view-parameter-inferred-from-builtin-argument
  suite: scenario
  spec: ["#interfaces", "§10.1", "#host-namespaces", "§16.1", "§16.5", "#clients", "§12.1"]
  tags: ["typing", "boundary"]
  note: '''
    §10.1's third inference anchor: "a typed function argument". A surface `$view`
    is database-evaluated (§16.5), so the only functions it can call are the
    built-in ones, whose signatures §16.1/§6.5 pin — `string.lower(text)` here.
    That makes the argument position decidable with no host resolution.

    `@q`'s only occurrence is inside that call. It is NOT a comparison operand
    (the operand of `==` is the call result, not the parameter) and not a key
    selector, so the built-in signature is the sole constraint on it. §10.1
    therefore types `@q` as `text` and the surface loads with no `$params` block.

    Attack: the anchor is indirect, so a client may hope an indirectly-inferred
    parameter is weaker than a directly-inferred or declared one and gets widened
    to `json` at the wire. §12.1 step 3 parses arguments against the inferred
    shape with no such gradation: the JSON number 5 does not decode as `text` and
    the request is malformed. (A built-in slot that pins no single type — `size`
    accepts text, bytes, a set, or a collection — constrains nothing instead, and
    keeps the explicit-declaration error of
    `10/surface-view-parameter-without-anchor-invalid`.)
  '''

  package: {
    $liasse: 1
    $app: "t.viewbuiltin@1.0.0"
    $model: {
      people: {
        $key: "id"
        id: "text"
        handle: "text"
      }
      $public: {
        by_handle: {
          // No $params: @q is inferred `text` from `string.lower(text)` (§16.1).
          $view: ".people[:p | p.handle == string.lower(@q)] { id, handle }"
        }
      }
    }
    $data: {
      people: {
        p1: { handle: "ada" }
        p2: { handle: "grace" }
      }
    }
  }

  steps: [
    { connect: "c1" }
    // The built-in lowercases the supplied argument before the comparison, so an
    // uppercase argument matches the stored lowercase handle.
    { watch: "public.by_handle", args: { q: "ADA" }, on: "c1", id: "w1",
      expect_init: { value: [ { id: "p1", handle: "ada" } ] } }
    { watch: "public.by_handle", args: { q: "Grace" }, on: "c1", id: "w2",
      expect_init: { value: [ { id: "p2", handle: "grace" } ] } }
    // The inferred `text` binds at the wire boundary exactly as a declared one.
    { watch: "public.by_handle", args: { q: 5 }, on: "c1", id: "w3",
      expect: { outcome: rejected, violates: ["#clients", "§12.1"] } }
  ]
}
